#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="Build every subset of the optional features"
#
# `lint` checks the two ENDS of the feature lattice — all on, all off — and they
# do not meet in the middle. That gap is not theoretical: gating
# `Node::bind_from_env` behind `env` made `--features blocking` alone stop
# compiling, because `blocking::Node::bind_from_env` calls it, and neither end
# noticed.
#
# `test --no-run`, not `check`. `check` compiled the library and NOT its
# `#[cfg(test)]` blocks, so a unit test calling a feature-gated function was
# invisible here: `cargo test -p keylink --no-default-features` failed to
# compile while this task stayed green. That is exactly the hole the task exists
# to close, and it had one — `authz.rs`'s `unset_env_fails_closed` called
# `Allowlist::from_env` with no gate of its own.
#
# `--lib` in the loop, and `--all-targets` ONCE at the bottom. This paragraph used
# to refuse the second half outright: the tests under `keylink/tests/` use the
# blocking API and `Body::from_file`, so they could not compile without the
# features that provide them, "and demanding it would only teach us to add feature
# gates to tests that legitimately want the feature".
#
# **That prediction was right about the mechanism and wrong about the price.** The
# gates were added, and what they bought is not tidiness. It is that `--all-targets`
# at the all-off end is now RUNNABLE, so a lint or an error that only appears
# without a feature is reachable in a test and an example for the first time.
# Before, the only thing any tool could say about `keylink/tests/` at that end was
# that it did not build.
#
# The inventory, if you want it, is derived rather than written down here — count
# ATTRIBUTES and not grep hits, because a comment quoting an attribute is not one:
#
#     grep -rhE '^\s*#!?\[cfg\(feature = "[a-z-]+"\)\]\s*$' keylink/tests/ | sort | uniq -c
#
# At the time of writing that gives 33 `persist-file`, 2 `blocking` and 1 `fs`
# across seven files, plus `required-features` on one example. Two of the 33 are
# whole-file `#![cfg]`, in `persist.rs` and `durable.rs`. **A first draft of this
# paragraph said "two tests and one `let`" for `blocking` and it is one test and
# one `let`** — the third hit was a comment quoting the attribute.
#
# The 24-build loop stays `--lib` and is not multiplied by targets. The lattice
# middle is a library question — a feature-gated item called from another
# feature's code — and paying 24 test-target builds to ask it again is the cost
# this task was shaped to avoid.
#
# What the gates cost. **TWO pairs of figures, because the obvious command and the
# interesting subset are not the same, and an earlier version of this paragraph
# quoted the subset under the command that reports the superset.**
#
#   cargo nextest list -p keylink --all-targets                     498 -> 466, 32 go
#   cargo nextest list -p keylink --all-targets -E 'kind(test)'      319 -> 292, 27 go
#
# each run once with `--no-default-features` and once without.
#
# The 27 are integration tests, one per gated target. **The other five are LIBRARY
# unit tests, and they leave for their own reasons** — `authz::tests::
# unset_env_fails_closed` needs `env`, and four `blocking::tests::*` need
# `blocking`. Those five were already gated before this branch; nothing here
# touched them. The lib holds 179 unit tests at default features and 174 without.
#
# So **32 is what leaves and 27 is what these gates account for.** Quote the wrong
# one and a reader who re-runs the obvious command gets 32 and concludes the record
# is short by five.
#
# Nothing appears at the all-off end that is absent at default, either way.
#
# The durability adapter multiplies this list by three rather than by four, and
# saying which three is the point. `persist-file` implies `persist`, so
# `persist-file` alone is not a subset anybody can build — the honest lattice is
# each of the eight above with the adapter off, with `persist`, and with
# `persist-file`. Twenty-four builds.
#
# It is not busywork on either axis. `persist` alone is the state this project
# claims compiles for a browser, and it is the only one of the three that
# `.mise/tasks/wasm` can check — so the middle column is what keeps the claim
# honest on a native host too, where an accidental `use keylink_persist::file`
# inside `src/persist.rs` would compile at the `persist-file` end and fail here.
set -euo pipefail

for f in "" blocking fs env blocking,fs blocking,env fs,env blocking,fs,env; do
    for p in "" persist persist-file; do
        # An empty subset and an empty adapter make an empty `--features`, which
        # cargo accepts; a comma between two empties does not.
        both="$(printf '%s\n' "$f,$p" | sed -e 's/^,//' -e 's/,$//')"
        echo "── features: ${both:-none}"
        cargo test -p keylink --lib --no-run --no-default-features --features "$both"
    done
done

# `keylink-persist` has one optional feature and both ends were built by
# nothing until `versioned.rs` existed: `lint` covers the all-on end through
# --all-features, and the off end had no builder at all. The frame and the
# migration chain need no adapter, and this is what keeps that true — a
# `use crate::file::..` slipped into `versioned.rs` compiles fine at the on end.
#
# **`--lib` excludes doctests, and that was a hole this task could not see.**
# The only example on `Versioned` — the crate's feature-independent core — began
# `use keylink_persist::file::Files`, so it had not compiled without the `file`
# feature since the day it was written. Nothing was red: this task ran `--lib`,
# `test` runs default features and `docs` runs `--all-features`. A doctest is
# compiled as its own crate against the built lib, so it is a second target and
# needs a second invocation. `--doc` cannot be combined with `--no-run`, so
# these actually run; there are five of them and they take a tenth of a second.
for f in "" file; do
    echo "── keylink-persist features: ${f:-none}"
    cargo test -p keylink-persist --lib --no-run --no-default-features --features "$f"
    cargo test -p keylink-persist --doc --no-default-features --features "$f"
done

# The same hole exists for `keylink`, whose doctests the loop above also cannot
# see. One run and not twenty-four: the failure being guarded is a doctest that
# names a feature-gated item, and the all-off end is where every one of those
# fails at once. `lint` covers the all-on end. The middle of the lattice is
# where `--lib` earns its twenty-four builds, and doctests do not multiply the
# same way — a doctest that needs `blocking` fails identically with `fs` on or
# off.
echo "── keylink doctests: no default features"
cargo test -p keylink --doc --no-default-features

# Every TARGET at the all-off end, once. This is the half the paragraph at the top
# of this file used to refuse, and it lives here rather than in `gate` for two
# reasons: this task already owns the all-off end, so it is not a seventeenth step,
# and by the time it runs the loop above has built keylink's dependency graph at
# this exact feature resolution 24 times over, so the dependencies are warm and
# what this compiles is keylink's own targets.
#
# `check` and not `test --no-run`: this asks whether the targets COMPILE at the
# all-off end, and it is `mise run test` that runs them at default features.
#
# It went green by adding feature gates and NOT by turning a feature on. That
# distinction is the whole value: `default-features = false` must keep meaning
# what a caller who writes it gets. `cargo check -p keylink --all-targets` at
# default features is separately green with no dead-code warning, which is what
# says the gates cover exactly the items that need them and not one more.
echo "── keylink every target: no default features"
cargo check -p keylink --all-targets --no-default-features
